# Line endings.
#
# Without this file the behaviour depends on each clone's core.autocrlf,
# which is a per-machine setting and therefore not a decision the project
# has made. That is where the constant "LF will be replaced by CRLF"
# warnings came from. The index has always held LF; this only writes that
# down so a second machine cannot decide otherwise.
* text=auto

# PowerShell 5.1 is the fussy one here. build-zip.ps1 already documents why
# it stays pure ASCII (a .ps1 without BOM is read as ANSI, and one stray en
# dash breaks parsing); CRLF is the matching half of that. Everything else
# in the tree is read by PHP, Node tooling or a browser, none of which care.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# .distignore and .gitattributes itself are read by build-zip.ps1 and git,
# both line-ending agnostic, but keep them normalised for the same reason.
.distignore  text
.gitattributes text

# Vendored libraries ship exactly as their upstream released them. They are
# generated artefacts, so diffing them line by line is noise rather than
# information.
assets/vendor/* -diff
